{
  "cases": [
    {
      "id": "C1",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA Part A identifies 100 grid puzzles, exact-grid scoring, and a narrow comparison purpose. For reliability/precision (section 9), repeated runs are an explicit reinterpretation of retesting an AI system; evidence is insufficient (0), rather than inadequate (1). Validity (section 10) cannot extend beyond the stated tasks.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "The report does not say whether runs were repeated.",
          "reason": "Repetition is unreported; it cannot be assumed either to have occurred or not occurred."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "70% in one reported run",
          "reason": "One reported score does not demonstrate consistency across runs."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C2",
      "instrument_review": "Part A describes a fixed grid-puzzle comparison with exact scoring. Reinterpreting EFPA retesting as repeated AI evaluations, section 9 has insufficient evidence (0) for score stability: the evaluation explicitly occurred once. This establishes an absent repeat study, not unstable performance.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "ran the evaluation exactly once and did not repeat it",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly rules out repeated evaluations."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "ran the evaluation exactly once and did not repeat it",
          "reason": "Without repeated scores, stability remains unknown."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C3",
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA section 9, repeated AI runs provide direct precision evidence for this system and setup. The observed range meets the report's stability definition. This is not a reliability coefficient, so EFPA coefficient thresholds should not be applied mechanically; other systems, conditions, and broader validity remain unestablished.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "repeated the evaluation ten times with the same system, inputs, instructions, resources, and settings",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly describes repeated evaluations under matching conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Scores ranged from 69% to 71%. The report defines stable for this comparison as a range of at most two percentage points.",
          "reason": "The observed two-point range meets the stated definition."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C4",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA section 9 distinguishes conducting a precision study from obtaining consistent scores. Repeated AI runs were conducted, but their range fails the stated stability standard, indicating inadequate precision for that standard. Exact scoring alone does not guarantee stable system performance.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "repeated the evaluation ten times with the same system, inputs, instructions, resources, and settings",
          "reason": "Repeated evaluations with matching conditions are explicitly reported."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "Scores ranged from 20% to 90%. The report defines stable for this comparison as a range of at most two percentage points.",
          "reason": "The observed range explicitly exceeds the stated stability limit."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C5",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA sections 6.2 and 6.3 distinguish documentation from measurement evidence. Installation guidance adds procedural information, but its adequacy cannot be fully assessed from this summary. Section 9 still has insufficient evidence (0) for repeated-run precision; installation instructions supply no new evaluation results.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "The report does not say whether runs were repeated.",
          "reason": "Installation guidance does not establish that repeated evaluations occurred."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "They report no additional evaluation results.",
          "reason": "No repeated-score evidence was added to support stability."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C6",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA Part A and section 10 align the stated purpose with a narrow observed comparison: A solved more evaluated puzzles. General intelligence would require evidence beyond this content. Norms need not be required for a solely raw-score ranking purpose under section 8.3.2.2; repeated-run precision remains unreported.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "narrow",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "System A solves 80 puzzles and system B solves 60 in the reported evaluation.",
          "reason": "A solved more puzzles in the reported comparison."
        },
        {
          "id": "broad",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Only grid puzzles were tested; no findings about other tasks are supplied.",
          "reason": "The evidence does not establish performance or intelligence across domains."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C7",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA section 10 evaluates validity against intended use. The observed grid-puzzle advantage supports the narrow comparison, but evidence is insufficient (0) for selecting by general intelligence across domains. Because validity for that selection is critical, EFPA guidance restricts such use to research or exceptional expert circumstances pending evidence; this does not prove the benchmark intrinsically poor.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "narrow",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "System A solves 80 puzzles and system B solves 60 in the reported evaluation.",
          "reason": "The reported results directly support A's advantage on these puzzles."
        },
        {
          "id": "broad",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Only grid puzzles were tested; no findings about other tasks are supplied.",
          "reason": "A broad intended use does not supply the missing cross-domain validity evidence."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C8",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA section 10 distinguishes initial test scores from evidence relating assistance to actual decision correctness. The randomized trial supplies relevant criterion evidence for this workshop, with 120 inspectors, blinded correctness checks, and an uncertainty interval favoring A. This supports the narrow intended use; it does not establish safety, time benefits, or generalization to other workshops. The trial interval does not itself establish benchmark reliability under section 9.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "before",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Initially, only test scores were available.",
          "reason": "Those scores alone do not establish an improvement in real workshop decisions."
        },
        {
          "id": "after",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "With A, 90% of decisions were correct; with B, 70%. The reported uncertainty interval for the difference was 12 to 28 percentage points.",
          "reason": "The randomized trial and positive uncertainty interval support better correctness with A in the studied setting."
        },
        {
          "id": "safety",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Time and safety outcomes were not measured.",
          "reason": "Improved decision correctness does not establish an unmeasured safety benefit."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "closing_note": "No unresolved instruction ambiguity. These are focused applications of the complete original EFPA framework, not full questionnaires. Across cases, Part A covers description, classification, scoring, reports, and supply; Part B covers rationale/documentation, materials, norms, precision, validity, fair use, reports, and final recommendations; Part C records sources. Unreported applicable qualities remain insufficiently evidenced, not demonstrably inadequate. Report availability is unknown, so report quality cannot be assessed. Human bodily prerequisites, participant debriefing, and psychological user qualifications are inapplicable to AI systems as written; AI interface requirements, operator guidance, and comparisons across relevant system groups are explicit reinterpretations, with no supporting evidence assumed. Exact-match scoring requires no inter-rater reliability where no scoring judgment occurs. Human inspector involvement in C8 is reported trial evidence, not a claim that this review experiment studies human participants. Sources used: only the supplied fictional passages and EFPA (2025), version 5.0, https://www.efpa.eu/, CC BY-SA 4.0. No aggregate benchmark-quality score or EFPA endorsement is implied."
}
